{"name":"Lyman Camellia","hex":"#e5c7b9","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"camellia","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any plant of the genus Camellia, shrubs and small trees native to Asia; Camellia japonica is the most popular as a garden plant; Camellia sinensis is the tea plant."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/camellia"}],"entries":[{"title":"Camellia","blocks":[{"text":"Camellia is a genus of flowering plants in the family Theaceae. They are found in tropical and subtropical areas in eastern and southern Asia, from the Himalayas east to Japan and Indonesia. There are more than 220 described species; almost all are found in southern China and Indochina. Camellias are popular ornamental, tea, and woody-oil plants cultivated worldwide for centuries. Over 26,000 cultivars, with more than 51,000 cultivar names, including synonyms, have been registered or published."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Camellia_sasanqua1JAM343.jpg/330px-Camellia_sasanqua1JAM343.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Camellia","wikidata":"Q212815"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Camellia_%C3%97_intermedia_fma._alba_%28Tuyama%29_Nagam._in_Fl._Japan_2a-_398_%282006%29_20240216_180127.jpg/500px-Camellia_%C3%97_intermedia_fma._alba_%28Tuyama%29_Nagam._in_Fl._Japan_2a-_398_%282006%29_20240216_180127.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Camellia_%C3%97_intermedia_fma._alba_%28Tuyama%29_Nagam._in_Fl._Japan_2a-_398_%282006%29_20240216_180127.jpg/1280px-Camellia_%C3%97_intermedia_fma._alba_%28Tuyama%29_Nagam._in_Fl._Japan_2a-_398_%282006%29_20240216_180127.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/